{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789412122,"nonce":"XWFMIFLinTR3fJxFFNJB2dP5nnI=","message":"Step 1 landed as 99549f6, step 2 as the commit after 7712e78. Transfer bench (200k iterations, load ~6), ns / B per crossing, before the cells → after both steps:\n\n| crossing | Haxe→Wren | Wren→Haxe |\n|---|---|---|\n| same object | 63 / 16 B → 52 / 0 B | 39 / 0 → 41 / 0 |\n| fresh object | 134 / 128 B → 125 / 144 B | 120 / 160 B → 105 / 192 B |\n| returned object | 130 / 160 B → 114 / 192 B | 118 / 128 B → 110 / 144 B |\n| sequence, per element | 55 / 0.2 B → 52 / 0 B | 98 / 16 B → 89 / 16 B |\n\nNo garbage on a crossing any more: a Haxe object crossing again is a map lookup for its cell (the 16 B wrapper is gone), a Wren object crossing again is a shadow read. A first crossing allocates the one cell, 80 bytes, in place of the wrapper plus stand-in instance plus its fields (Haxe→Wren) or the ref plus face (Wren→Haxe); the bytes per new object went up by 16–32 because the cell carries both views whichever side made it. Swarm mixed frame unchanged, 1.78 ms.\n\nLayout as landed (80 B): descriptor, bridge word, wren_lift ObjInstance (40 B, at +16 where the prefix puts it), object value, front, flags. Ash's cells: descriptor with the Haxe protocol, found by object address in the cell module's map, entry dropped by the cell's drop hook. Wren's view: written by proxy on the first crossing (class = the installed class, no fields), the cell held by address plus 16.\n\nWhat step 2 needed beyond the layout, found by the imports test's identity check after System.gc():\n\n- Wren's marking marks a cell in its bridge word as it marks its own prefix, but a cell is no pin, so collect_end never claimed it for the core and the core freed it while Wren held it. The heap keeps a `views` list of cells Wren holds (VIEW_HELD flag in the bridge word), the anchor retains them between cycles as it retains pins, collect_end claims the marked ones and drops the rest.\n- A cell only on a native frame during a cycle was not found by scan_range (which resolves this record's allocations only); it looks for held cells too.\n- With nothing allocated on Wren's side, no cycle ran: 500 MB live after 2M fresh crossings, every cell retained by the anchor. A held cell now counts toward bytes_since_cycle and handing a view out goes through made (finish_alloc), the same safepoint an allocation is.\n\nRemaining from the plan: step 3, Ash's hl_type trailer (the user's call), which would drop the mirror and the Haxe-side map. Not covered by a stress run: ASH_GC_STRESS and WLIFT_GC_STRESS crash the interop tests since wren_lift's VM split (16d8592), before and after the cells.","files":null}]}